Q: JPA @JoinColumn issues while joining on non primary key columns A: <p>You should use right @Id annotation on the joined Entity(table) property(column), which you want to join by. The best solution is to normalize the tables in the DB, and make the appropriate changes in your Entities.</p>
<pre><code>@Data
@Entity
@Table(name = &quot;person&quot;)
class Person {

    @Id
    String person_sk;

    int person_id;

    String Person_name;

    @OneToMany(cascade = CascadeType.ALL)
    @JoinColumn(name = &quot;person_sk&quot;)
    List&lt;address&gt; getAddresses() {}
}

@Data
@Entity
@Table(name = &quot;address&quot;)
class Address {

    @Id
    int person_reference_id;

    int address_id;

    int phone_id;

    String street_name, zip_code;
}
</code></pre>

Q: React Native Firebase Firestore getDoc() hangs indefinitely after setDoc() A: <p><strong>Title: React Native Firebase Firestore <code>getDoc</code> hangs indefinitely after <code>setDoc</code></strong></p>
<p>It's likely that the issue isn't with <code>getDoc</code> itself, but with how the Firestore SDK handles the transition between the mutation and the read. Here are the most common causes for this &quot;hanging&quot; behavior in React Native:</p>
<p><strong>1. Async/Await and Promise resolution</strong> Ensure you are correctly awaiting both calls. Firestore SDK can sometimes hang if an unhandled promise is floating around during a navigation transition or component unmount.</p>
<pre><code>try {
  await setDoc(docRef, data);
  // Important: Firestore local cache might need a split second to sync
  // Try adding a tiny delay or ensuring the read is independent
  const docSnap = await getDoc(docRef); 

  if (docSnap.exists()) {
    console.log(&quot;Document data:&quot;, docSnap.data());
  }
} catch (error) {
  console.error(&quot;Firestore operation failed:&quot;, error);
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>2. Firestore Offline Persistence</strong> If you have <code>enableIndexedDbPersistence</code> (web) or default persistence enabled, the SDK might be trying to sync with the local cache while you are forcing a read. Check if the issue persists if you disable persistence for testing:</p>
<p>// For testing purposes, disable persistence to see if it fixes the hang</p>
<p>await disableNetwork(db);</p>
<p><strong>3. React Native Metro/Debugger</strong> Sometimes, the <strong>Remote Debugger (Chrome)</strong> in React Native interferes with WebSocket connections used by Firebase. If you have &quot;Debug JS Remotely&quot; enabled, try disabling it. Firestore's gRPC/Websocket traffic often breaks in the remote debugger.</p>
<p><strong>4. Check for Component Unmounting</strong> If you are calling this in a <code>useEffect</code> and the component unmounts before <code>getDoc</code> resolves, it can cause weird behavior. Add a flag to prevent updates:</p>
<pre><code>useEffect(() =&gt; {
  let isMounted = true;
  const fetchData = async () =&gt; {
    await setDoc(...);
    const docSnap = await getDoc(...);
    if (isMounted) { /* update state */ }
  };
  fetchData();
  return () =&gt; { isMounted = false; };
}, []);
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Try removing the debugger first. 90% of &quot;hanging&quot; issues in React Native Firebase are due to the network stack clashing with the Chrome Debugger.</p>
